Remote shell (rshd) is DISABLED by default (security).

The remote shell daemon accepts command execution from TRUSTED network peers.
Even with its sandbox, trust gate, and CPU/memory budget, remote code execution
should be a deliberate opt-in, not a default-on service — so TOS ships this
`.disabled` marker beside 20-rshd.lua.

Effect: the service is REGISTERED (so `service` can see it) but not started at
boot. To enable it:

    service start 20-rshd      # starts it now AND removes this marker,
                               # so it auto-starts on future boots

To disable it again:

    service stop 20-rshd
    # then recreate this marker (touch /etc/rc.d/20-rshd.disabled) so it
    # stays off across reboots.

Review the trust list (`net trust`) before exposing a machine.
